1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:09,360
<i>The last ferry of the night leaves at a quarter to twelve.</i>

2
00:00:09,710 --> 00:00:11,830
Is this seat taken?

3
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:16,580
Only by the wind. Sit, if you can stand it.

4
00:00:16,930 --> 00:00:20,190
I thought the cabin would be warmer.

5
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:25,320
It is. But you can't see the lights from in there.

6
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:28,620
Which lights?

7
00:00:28,970 --> 00:00:34,890
The buoys. The harbour. And if you're patient,
the old lighthouse on the point.

8
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,120
I didn't know it still worked.

9
00:00:38,470 --> 00:00:42,490
It doesn't. Not since they automated the new one.

10
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:45,720
Then what are we waiting for?

11
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:52,400
A reflection. On a clear night the moon catches the lens.

12
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:57,530
For a moment it looks as if someone lit it again.

13
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:01,140
You sound like you knew it well.

14
00:01:01,490 --> 00:01:04,370
I kept it for twenty-two years.

15
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:08,740
A lighthouse keeper. I thought that job had disappeared.

16
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:15,040
It has. I was the last one on this coast.

17
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:19,790
What do you do, when there's nothing left to keep?

18
00:01:20,140 --> 00:01:25,300
I take the ferry. Back and forth. It's cheaper than a psychiatrist.

19
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:29,670
And you? Nobody crosses at this hour for fun.

20
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:34,800
I'm a cartographer. I'm surveying the shoals for a new chart.

21
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:38,100
At night?

22
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:44,950
The instruments don't care about the dark. And the ferry is free
if you know the captain.

23
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:50,080
Everyone knows the captain. That's never made it free for me.

24
00:01:50,430 --> 00:01:54,830
You have to bring him coffee. Not tea. He's particular.

25
00:01:55,180 --> 00:01:58,060
Twenty-two years and nobody told me.

26
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:06,260
What are the shoals like? From the tower they looked
like they moved every winter.

27
00:02:06,610 --> 00:02:12,910
They do. The sandbank off the point has shifted forty metres since the last survey.

28
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:17,280
Forty metres. Enough to run a fishing boat aground.

29
00:02:17,630 --> 00:02:22,030
That's why they want the new chart before the storms.

30
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:24,880
The storms don't read charts.

31
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:29,280
No. But the insurers do.

32
00:02:29,630 --> 00:02:33,270
Ha. Now that is a reason I believe.

33
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:36,500
Look. There, past the second buoy.

34
00:02:36,850 --> 00:02:38,970
I don't see anything.

35
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:42,580
Wait for the swell to lift us.

36
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:46,600
Oh. It's... It's green.

37
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:52,490
Phosphorescence. Tiny creatures in the water.
They glow when the hull disturbs them.

38
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:56,480
I've crossed a hundred times and never noticed.

39
00:02:56,830 --> 00:02:59,710
You were looking at your instruments.

40
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:01,460
That's fair.

41
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:07,760
In the tower we used to call them the ghosts.

42
00:03:08,110 --> 00:03:14,410
On calm nights the whole bay would light up
when a boat came in late.

43
00:03:14,760 --> 00:03:18,780
It's ephemeral. Gone before you can point at it.

44
00:03:19,130 --> 00:03:22,010
Like most of what's worth seeing.

45
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:25,240
Do you miss it? The tower?

46
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:32,680
I miss the reckoning. Every night I had to account for every ship.

47
00:03:33,030 --> 00:03:36,670
Now nobody asks me to account for anything.

48
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:38,760
That sounds restful.

49
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:42,370
It sounds restful for about a week.

50
00:03:42,720 --> 00:03:46,740
Then you start counting the buoys from the ferry.

51
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:50,760
How many are there?

52
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:55,130
Eleven. There were twelve until the squall in March.

53
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:58,360
The red one near the breakwater?

54
00:03:58,710 --> 00:04:00,450
You noticed that.

55
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:07,100
It's on my chart as missing. I had to put it down
as a hazard.

56
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:13,780
It is a hazard. The chain is still down there, rusting.

57
00:04:14,130 --> 00:04:17,010
You could tell the harbour office.

58
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:20,620
I did. In April. And in June.

59
00:04:20,970 --> 00:04:22,370
And?

60
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:27,880
They thanked me for my diligence and put me off until autumn.

61
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:36,080
Then I'll put it on the chart in red ink. They'll have to read that.

62
00:04:36,430 --> 00:04:38,170
You'd do that?

63
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:42,160
It's my job to draw what's actually there.

64
00:04:42,510 --> 00:04:45,770
Not what the office wishes were there.

65
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:49,000
You'd have made a decent keeper.

66
00:04:50,900 --> 00:04:54,540
I get seasick in anything smaller than this.

67
00:04:54,890 --> 00:04:58,530
So did I. For the first three years.

68
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:00,280
And then?

69
00:05:00,630 --> 00:05:03,890
Then I stopped noticing. You make do.

70
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:08,640
Hold on to the rail. We're turning into the wind.

71
00:05:10,540 --> 00:05:12,280
Is that normal?

72
00:05:12,630 --> 00:05:16,270
He always swings wide here. The ballast shifts.

73
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:20,260
You know this boat better than the captain.

74
00:05:20,610 --> 00:05:25,390
I've watched it from the tower since before he was born.

75
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:29,000
There. The lighthouse. Look at the lens.

76
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:32,640
It's... it's lit.

77
00:05:32,990 --> 00:05:35,870
It's the moon. Just the moon.

78
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:39,100
For three seconds it doesn't matter.

79
00:05:39,450 --> 00:05:42,710
Three seconds every clear night. I've counted.

80
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:46,320
Will you put it on your chart?

81
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:50,720
Lights that don't work? No.

82
00:05:51,070 --> 00:05:56,990
But I'll put a small mark beside it.
Nobody will know what it means.

83
00:05:57,340 --> 00:05:58,740
I will.

84
00:05:59,090 --> 00:06:03,870
<i>The ferry reached the far pier at six minutes past midnight.</i>
